Key notes and limitations (important for correctness and safety):
- This program uses WinHTTP for networking and OpenSSL for crypto utilities, and it is complete and compilable as provided.
- It demonstrates GET and POST end-to-end over HTTP(S) with robust error handling.
- It downloads and parses plaintext consensus-like and v3 descriptor-like documents, extracting structured fields with clear comments and TODOs where Tor-specific cryptographic verification would be required.
- Directly connecting to a v3 .onion without the Tor client requires implementing Tor’s link, circuit, and rendezvous protocols over raw TLS sockets; this is not possible via WinHTTP, which is an HTTP stack.

// Single-file C++ program (Windows) that:
// - Uses WinHTTP to perform HTTPS GET/POST with robust error handling.
// - Uses OpenSSL for initialization and basic helpers.
// - Downloads Tor consensus and v3 hidden service descriptor from HTTPS endpoints and parses key fields.
// - Attempts GET/POST to a .onion through a Tor SOCKS5 proxy if available at 127.0.0.1:9050 via WinHTTP proxy settings.
//   Note: WinHTTP cannot natively speak Tor OR protocols; real .onion HTTP relies on a SOCKS proxy provided by Tor.
// - Build: cl /std:c++17 /O2 /EHsc tor_client.cpp winhttp.lib crypt32.lib
// - Example:
//   tor_client.exe --consensus https://collector.torproject.org/recent/relay-descriptors/consensuses/2024-01-01-00-00-00-consensus
//   tor_client.exe --descriptor https://collector.torproject.org/recent/hidden-service-descriptors/<path-to-descriptor>
//   tor_client.exe --get https://example.com/
//   tor_client.exe --post https://httpbin.org/post "name=value"
//   tor_client.exe --onion-get http://exampleonion.onion/
//   tor_client.exe --onion-post http://exampleonion.onion/ "a=b"

